<h2 id="bring-back-the-complete-effect">Bring back the complete effect</h2>
<p>The restored candidate has three available places. Copying B203’s row without decrementing capacity would overstate what remains. Decrementing again on every retry would consume places without new bookings. Reconciliation needs one transaction covering the booking, the capacity change and the stable request outcome.</p>
<p>In the native fixture, the first application returned <code>created</code>. Repeating the same key and payload returned <code>existing</code>; reusing the key for a different customer raised <code>request payload conflict</code>. The administrator performed this reconciliation serially in the isolated candidate. Its small helper is not offered as a concurrent general-purpose idempotency protocol.</p>
<table><caption>The executed recovery and reconciliation</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">History</th><th scope="col">Capacity</th><th scope="col">Remaining</th><th scope="col">B203</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original source after mistake and booking</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>One</td></tr><tr><td>Candidate paused before mistake</td><td>3</td><td>3</td><td>Absent</td></tr><tr><td>First reconciliation</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>One</td></tr><tr><td>Same request repeated</td><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>One</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>External effects require separate evidence. A class confirmation email delivered before recovery is not undone by removing its outbox row. A provider charge is not refunded by rewinding a database. Reconcile effect identities against their own authorities before resending, suppressing or compensating. This fixture generated no external effects, so its recorded external acceptance is limited to that fact.</p>
<p>After inspection, the controller resumed replay, allowing targeted recovery to finish, then closed the candidate’s booking gate while reconciliation ran. The old source remained stopped and fenced. The final acceptance found one B203 for Edda, one accepted request identity, capacity 3 and remaining capacity 2. It deliberately left the candidate closed to new admissions; authority transfer in a real service follows acceptance of all those boundaries.</p>
<p>The <a href="/review/relational-collection/evidence/r37/restore-drill.py/" download>complete restore drill</a> creates its own source, base, local archive and candidates, verifies endpoint ownership, exercises the missing-WAL and fence-race counterexamples, and removes its disposable instances. Run it from an empty directory with Python 3 and the stated PostgreSQL 18.6 binary directory available. It uses local trust authentication only inside the private socket directory and is an instructional mechanism test, rather than a deployment backup service.</p>

<h2 id="retain-the-whole-route-to-service">Retain the whole route to service</h2>
<p>Backup retention is a graph of dependencies. An increment needs its ancestors; a target needs the required contiguous WAL and timeline history. Configuration, extension libraries, keys and the authority to retrieve the copies can be dependencies too. Removing an old base because a newer increment exists may remove every usable route through that increment.</p>
<p>A recovery objective needs a test starting from the copies and access you would actually possess after the failure. Include retrieval, provisioning, combination if needed, replay, application checks, reconciliation and authority transfer in the time to acceptable service. This local drill reports neither an RPO nor an RTO. Its several directories prove no independence from host or storage failure.</p>
<p>A physical standby can shorten the route to another running copy, but it also replays mistakes. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/replication/">Physical replicas and promotion</a> follows which copy may take authority. Moving the history into a new major version introduces different compatibility and cutover questions, developed in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/upgrades/">Upgrading and migrating PostgreSQL</a>.</p>

